The '''Koenigsegg CCX''' is a [[Mid-engine design|mid-engine]] [[sports car]] manufactured by Swedish automotive manufacturer [[Koenigsegg|Koenigsegg Automotive AB]]. The project began with the aim of making a global car, designed and engineered to comply with global safety and environment regulations, particularly to enter the [[Passenger vehicles in the United States|United States car market]]. To sell cars in the US, many alterations were made to the design of the CCR; the previously used [[Ford Modular engine]] was replaced by an in-house developed Koenigsegg engine designed to run on 91&nbsp;[[octane rating|octane]] fuel, readily available in the United States, and to meet Californian emission standards.<ref name="autoweek">{{cite web|url=https://www.autoweek.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=/20061030/FREE/61027006|title=2006 Koenigsegg CCX|publisher=[[Autoweek]]|access-date=6 September 2007|last=Vaughn|first=Mark|date=30 October 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927004321/http://www.autoweek.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=%2F20061030%2FFREE%2F61027006|archive-date=27 September 2007|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="design elements">{{cite web|url=http://www.koenigsegg.com/thecars/ccx.asp?ccx=2|title=Design Elements|publisher=[[Koenigsegg]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070811013612/http://www.koenigsegg.com/thecars/ccx.asp?ccx=2|archive-date=11 August 2007|url-status=dead|access-date=1 September 2007}}</ref>

The name ''CCX'' is an abbreviation for ''Competition Coupé X'', the X commemorating the 10th anniversary (X being the [[Roman numeral]] for ten) of the completion and test drive of the first [[Koenigsegg CC|CC]] prototype in 1996.<ref name="the CCX">{{cite web|url=http://www.koenigsegg.com/thecars/ccx.asp |title=The new CCX |publisher=[[Koenigsegg]] |access-date=31 August 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070811013759/http://www.koenigsegg.com/thecars/ccx.asp |archive-date=11 August 2007 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Overview==
The CCX was unveiled at the [[Geneva Motor Show#2006|2006 Geneva Motor Show]], sporting body modifications to meet US vehicle regulations and a new in-house developed 4.7L twin [[supercharged]] [[V8 engine]] capable of generating a maximum power output of {{cvt|806|PS|kW hp|0}} at 7,000 rpm and {{convert|920|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of torque at 5,500 rpm while running on 91 [[octane rating|octane]] gasoline.

===Powertrain===
The new engine is of all-aluminium construction made out of 356 aluminium with a [[Aluminium alloy#Temper designation|T7]] heat treatment to further enhance block integrity and cylinder bore chill during casting. Specifically created and cast for Koenigsegg by Grainger & Worrall, a casting specialist with [[Formula One|F1]] experience in drivetrain components, the engine was built, assembled and tested at their Ängelholm production plant.<ref name="design elements"/> The engine is lubricated with a [[dry sump]] system with a separate oil pump and the pistons are cooled by means of an internal cooler that sprays oil onto them in order to run high cylinder pressure with 91 octane fuel making it capable of {{convert|14|mpgus|abbr=on}} in combined cycle and {{convert|18|mpgus|abbr=on}} in highway travel.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.koenigsegg.com/models.php?model_submenu=specs&img=1 |title=Official website of the Swedish super sports car manufacturer |publisher=Koenigsegg |access-date=26 February 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100213160028/http://www.koenigsegg.com/models.php?model_submenu=specs&img=1 |archive-date=13 February 2010 }}</ref> Available transmissions are a CIMA 6-speed manual and a 6-speed [[sequential manual transmission]]. Power is fed to the wheels through a torque-sensitive [[limited-slip differential]].<ref name="CCX Specifications">{{cite web|url=http://www.koenigsegg.com/thecars/ccx.asp?ccx=2|title=CCX Specifications|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070811013612/http://www.koenigsegg.com/thecars/ccx.asp?ccx=2|archive-date=11 August 2007|url-status=dead|access-date=22 June 2012}}</ref>

===Body===
The chassis is made from [[carbon fibre reinforced plastic|carbon fibre]] reinforced with kevlar and aluminium honeycomb like the previous models. While the body keeps the [[targa top]] body style and the dihedral synchro-helix actuation doors,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.koenigsegg.com/thecars/design.asp?design=4 |title=The Doors |publisher=[[Koenigsegg]] |access-date=5 September 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070807220358/http://www.koenigsegg.com/thecars/design.asp?design=4 |archive-date=7 August 2007 |url-status=dead }}</ref> it is completely reworked. There is a new front bumper design, enhanced brake cooling, fog lamps, US patented head lamps, a new fresh air intake on the bonnet that acts as ram air booster, air intakes behind the front wheels to enhance airflow and a glass window over the engine.

The CCX has a frontal area of {{convert|2894|sqin|m2|abbr=on}} and a {{Cd|long=yes|link=car|0.32}}, with a CdA of {{cvt|0.56|m2|sqft}}. It also has a flat underside with [[Venturi effect|venturi tunnels]] at the rear and an optional rear [[spoiler (automotive)|spoiler]] to improve downforce.<ref name="CCX Specifications"/> At {{cvt|200|km/h|mph|0}} there is {{convert|60|kg|abbr=on}} of [[downforce]] over the front axle and {{cvt|65|kg|lb|0}} over the rear.<ref name="piston">{{cite web|url=http://www.pistonheads.com/roadtests/KoenigseggCCX.htm|title=Koenigsegg CCX|publisher=Piston Heads|date=17 August 2006|first=Ian|last=Kuah|access-date=7 September 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927225957/http://www.pistonheads.com/roadtests/KoenigseggCCX.htm|archive-date=27 September 2007|url-status=dead}}</ref> The car is {{convert|88|mm|in|abbr=on}} longer to comply with the US rear impact regulations and to free space around the rear muffler. On the interior, there is {{cvt|50|mm|in|0}} of extra headroom as well as specifically designed [[Sparco]] carbon fibre seats.<ref name= "CCX Press Release">{{cite web|url=http://www.koenigsegg.com.au/23-koenigsegg-ccx-press-release.htm|title=Koenigsegg CCX Press Release|access-date=22 June 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110914080147/http://www.koenigsegg.com.au/23-koenigsegg-ccx-press-release.htm|archive-date=14 September 2011|url-status=dead}}</ref>

