===Wheels and brakes===
First in the industry carbon fibre wheels are optional equipment, {{convert|3|kg|lb}} lighter than the standard forged alloy ''telephone-dial'' wheels, both using centre locking nuts. Diameter is 19&nbsp;inches at the front and 20&nbsp;inches at the rear equipped with 255/35&nbsp;Y19 front, 335/30&nbsp;Y20 rear Michelin Pilot Sport 2 tyres, 8 piston caliper [[Disc brake#Ceramic composites|carbon ceramic brakes]] measuring {{convert|380|mm|in|abbr=on}} in diameter at the front and 6 piston caliper {{convert|362|mm|in|abbr=on}} at the rear are optional, saving another {{convert|2|kg|lb|abbr=on}} of unsprung weight.<ref name="design elements"/><ref name="CCX Specifications"/>

==Variants==

===CCXR===
[[File:CCXR in London.jpg|thumb|right|Rear view of the light blue [[Al-Thani]] family CCXR Special One in [[London]]]]

{{Listen|filename=Koenigsegg CCX-R.ogg|title=Koenigsegg CCX-R|description=Koenigsegg CCX-R at Goodwood Festival of Speed 2009}}

The '''CCXR''' is a more "environmentally friendly" version of the CCX, powered by the same engine, but converted to use E85 and E100 [[ethanol fuel]], as well as standard 98 octane petrol.<ref name="tgmag">{{Cite news|last=Grunet|first=Peter|title=Herbal Essence|newspaper=[[Top Gear Magazine]]|pages=138–142|date=1 June 2007}}</ref> The CCXR required modified [[fuel injector]]s, upgraded fuel lines and piston rings, and a higher boost setting on the superchargers. In standard form, bodywork of the CCXR features heavily optimized aerodynamics, along with optional diffusers to enhance airflow, including an optional front [[Diffuser (automotive)#Splitters|splitter]] with an aerodynamic [[nolder]].<ref name="egg">{{cite web
  | title = CCXR Koenigsegg
  | publisher = Koenigsegg.com
  | url = https://www.koenigsegg.com/ccxr/
  | access-date = 14 January 2018
  | archive-date = 15 January 2018
  | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20180115071719/https://www.koenigsegg.com/ccxr/
  | url-status = dead
  }}</ref><ref name="carmag">{{cite web|url=http://www.carmagazine.co.uk/Green-Cars/Search-Results/Green-First-Pictures/Koenigsegg-CCXR/?&R=EPI-3621|title=A green Koenigsegg? Surely some mistake?|publisher=Car Magazine|access-date=5 September 2007|date=3 June 2007|last=Whitworth|first=Ben|archive-date=8 March 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120308172813/http://www.carmagazine.co.uk/Green-Cars/Search-Results/Green-First-Pictures/Koenigsegg-CCXR/|url-status=live}}</ref> When run on ethanol, the power increases to {{convert|1018|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}} at 7000 rpm and {{convert|1060|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of torque at 5600 rpm. This is a direct result of the cooling properties of [[ethanol]] in the engine's combustion chambers along with the added boost, made possible by ethanol's higher [[octane rating]] when compared to gasoline.<ref name="tgmag"/><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.koenigsegg.com/geneva2007/index.asp?menu=4 |title=CCXR Technical Specifications |publisher=[[Koenigsegg]] |access-date=5 September 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071025011508/http://www.koenigsegg.com/geneva2007/index.asp?menu=4 |archive-date=25 October 2007 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Due to the lower specific energy content of ethanol, the CCXR burns slightly more fuel than the CCX with a combined fuel consumption of {{convert|22|L/100 km|abbr=on}} under the EU cycle.

===CCX Edition, CCXR Edition, CCXR Special Edition===
At the [[Geneva Motor Show#2008|2008 Geneva Motor Show]], Koenigsegg presented two special edition models, the CCX Edition and the CCXR Edition, both fitted with a remapped, 4.8&nbsp;L twin-supercharged [[V8 engine|V8]] engine and limited to 2 and 4 units respectively.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.carsuk.net/exclusive-koenigsegg-cuts-production-numbers-on-the-ccx-ccxr-edition/ |title=Exclusive: Koenigsegg cuts production numbers on the CCX & CCXR Edition | work =Cars UK |date=19 March 2009 | archive-date=25 April 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090425035831/http://www.carsuk.net/exclusive-koenigsegg-cuts-production-numbers-on-the-ccx-ccxr-edition/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The modifications to the engine increase the power of the CCX Edition to {{convert|900|PS|kW hp|0|abbr=on}} on normal gasoline and {{convert|940|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of torque while the CCXR Edition ratings remained unchanged over the CCXR.

The Edition models are more track oriented compared to the standard models, being equipped with stiffer springs and anti-roll bars, new dampers and a lowered chassis, a bare carbon body, unique 11-spoke aluminium wheels with a unique rear wing, and a larger front splitter and side winglets, all of which make the car capable of producing {{convert|350|kg|lb|0|abbr=on}} of downforce at {{convert|250|kph|mph|0|abbr=on}}.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.koenigsegg.com/models/ccxr-edition/|title=CCXR Edition specifications|access-date=24 May 2012|archive-date=12 February 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150212085413/http://www.koenigsegg.com/models/ccxr-edition/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.koenigsegg.com/models/ccx-edition/|title=CCX Edition specifications|access-date=24 May 2012|archive-date=14 February 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150214022207/http://www.koenigsegg.com/models/ccx-edition/|url-status=dead}}</ref> The interior is also reworked and features; colour matched leather carpets, Koenigsegg Edition side step plates and Edition chronograph instrument cluster. All other extra equipment for the Koenigsegg CCX and CCXR Edition comes as standard which include optional carbon fibre wheels, a rearview camera, Satnav or Bluetooth, amplifiers, and an exhaust system made entirely from [[inconel]].

Later, Koenigsegg also built two CCXR Special Edition cars which when compared to the old CCXR Edition had equipped with the double F1 wing, a revised aero package with a unique and larger front splitter and side winglets, wheel vents on top of the body, as well as Koenigsegg's first F1 Paddleshift system.<ref>{{cite web |title=CCXR Special Edition |url=https://www.koenigsegg.com/car/ccxr-special-edition/ |website=koenigsegg.com |access-date=20 May 2020 |archive-date=16 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190416162209/https://www.koenigsegg.com/car/ccxr-special-edition/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

