==Motorsport==
[[File:White Koenigsegg CCGT Goodwood 2007.jpg|right|thumbnail|250px|Only existing CCGT at Goodwood 2007]]

In order to compete in the [[FIA GT Championship]] Koenigsegg created the '''CCGT''' race car, based on the production CC model range; making its debut appearance at the [[Geneva Motor Show#2007|2007 Geneva Motor Show]] and built to comply with the [[Automobile Club de l'Ouest|ACO]] and [[Fédération Internationale de l'Automobile|FIA]] regulations for the GT1 class.<ref name="Autoblog: Koenigsegg drops a duo">{{cite web|url=http://www.autoblog.com/2007/03/02/koenigsegg-drops-a-duo-ccgt-and-1018-hp-biofuel-ccxr/|title=Autoblog: Koenigsegg drops a duo|date=2 March 2007 |access-date=24 May 2012|archive-date=19 July 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110719175644/http://www.autoblog.com/2007/03/02/koenigsegg-drops-a-duo-ccgt-and-1018-hp-biofuel-ccxr/|url-status=live}}</ref>

The CCGT's engine is based on the unit used in the Koenigsegg CCX with the superchargers removed and the capacity increased to 5.0&nbsp;L to compensate for the loss of power.<ref name="Autoblog: Koenigsegg drops a duo"/> The CCGT had over 600&nbsp;kg downforce and over 600&nbsp;hp normally aspirated, racing restricted hp.<ref>{{cite web |title=CCGT |url=https://www.koenigsegg.com/car/ccgt/ |website=koenigsegg.com |access-date=20 May 2020 |archive-date=16 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190416162044/https://www.koenigsegg.com/car/ccgt/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> Due to the already lightweight construction of the road-going model it was based on, the weight was easily reduced under the minimum {{convert|1100|kg|lb|0|abbr=on}}, which means that the [[Ballast weight|Ballast]] can be placed optimally in order to meet the mandatory weight.

Two months after the car completed its first shakedown runs, the FIA GT1 regulations were suddenly changed, according to the altered regulations, carbon monocoques were disallowed and there had to be a minimum of 350 road cars produced per year of the model that was to compete, something that Koenigsegg was unable to achieve, prohibiting the CCGT from racing.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.koenigsegg.com/models/ccgt/|title=Koenigsegg CCGT specifications|access-date=24 May 2012|archive-date=21 February 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150221152749/http://www.koenigsegg.com/models/ccgt/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

On 14 July 2023, the CCGT was sold for £3,319,000 (inclusive of buyer's premium) in Bonhams Goodwood Festival of Speed auction.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Jupp |first1=Ethan |title=£3.3million Koenigsegg CCGT headlines the Bonhams Festival of Speed sale results |url=https://www.goodwood.com/grr/event-coverage/festival-of-speed/2023/7/koenigsegg-ccgt-headlines-the-bonhams-festival-of-speed-sale/ |website=www.goodwood.com |access-date=3 November 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Smith |first1=Lawrence |title=Goodwood Festival of Speed 2023 auction: stunning race car was 'undoubted highlight' of 30th anniversary event, says Bonhams Cars |url=https://www.sussexexpress.co.uk/news/people/goodwood-festival-of-speed-2023-auction-stunning-race-car-was-undoubted-highlight-of-30th-anniversary-event-says-bonhamscars-4219994 |website=www.sussexexpress.co.uk |date=15 July 2023 |access-date=3 November 2023}}</ref>

